      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "Configuration design, or the placement and orienting of components in a system, is an essential aspect of the engineering design process for many systems, and choices made in this stage can have a large downstream impact on cost and performance. Spacecraft are one such system, with many components that must be placed according to competing goals and requirements. Due to the complex and multi-domain nature of the problem, spacecraft configuration design currently relies heavily on the intuition and experience of a team of engineers. The problem addressed is, given a list of components and their characteristics (mass, dimensions, type, etc.), produce locations and orientations for each component which optimize on some given objectives. To solve this, a transformer model trained using a deep reinforcement learning (deep RL) implementation, Proximal Policy Optimization, is used. The transformer is benchmarked against a multi-objective genetic algorithm, NSGA-II, and random search. Results show the transformer model expands the Pareto front significantly more quickly than the genetic algorithm and the random search. This method could be used by spacecraft design teams to quickly perform a thorough tradespace exploration of the configuration design space. Additionally, with an appropriate set of components and objective and constraint functions, this method could be applied to other configuration design problems, such as chiplet design.",
